   "text": "I read this as a fusidic-acid-type skeleton and worked out each stereocentre from the wedges and hashes in the drawing.\n\n**Skeleton**\n- Side chain: (CH3)2C=CH\u2013CH2\u2013CH2\u2013C(COOH)=, a prenyl-type chain ending in a carboxylic acid on an exocyclic double bond.\n- Ring D is a five-membered ring carrying the =C(COOH) carbon. It has an acetate ester (OAc) on the carbon next to that alkene carbon, a quaternary carbon bearing a methyl, and a CH.\n- Rings C, B and A are six-membered rings fused in sequence.\n  - Ring C carries a secondary OH.\n  - Two quaternary carbons carry methyls.\n  - Ring A carries a 3-OH and a 4-methyl.\n\n**Stereochemistry**\nI assigned each centre from the wedge or hash on it (the OAc, both OH groups, the methyls and the ring-junction hydrogens), then converted to @/@@ in SMILES atom order. The exocyclic C=C comes out trans between the chain CH2 and the ring CH(OAc) carbon. Equivalently, the COOH sits on the same side as the CH(OAc) carbon, which is the Z configuration of fusidic acid.\n\nI derived the SMILES by hand from the drawing, so the @/@@ assignments carry some risk of a slip.\n\nSMILES: CC(C)=CCC/C(C(=O)O)=C1/[C@@H](OC(C)=O)C[C@@]2(C)[C@H]1C[C@@H](O)[C@@H]1[C@]2(C)CC[C@H]3[C@H](C)[C@H](O)CC[C@]13C"
